WEBVTT

STYLE
::cue(c.color9CD5FF) { color: #9CD5FF; }

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.000 line:5% align:center
<b><c.color9CD5FF>One Pace Addon</c></b>

2
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:45.420
<i>See the light I have</i>
<i>searched for everywhere!</i>

3
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.730
My money says that
we live through this.

4
00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.440
A cloud road that
goes straight up?

5
00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.730
You might wanna
reconsider that bet.

6
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.730
[USOPP, CHOPPER scream]

7
00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.300
I wonder if this is a natural
formation of the clouds.

8
00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.490
There's nothing natural
about this at all!

9
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.460
[laughs]

10
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:19.350
[gasps]

11
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:20.400
[CHOPPER]
Look! The exit!

12
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.360
They put up a big
sign showing us!

13
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:23.650
[ROBIN]
Godland?

14
00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:26.120
What is Godland Skypiea?

15
00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.700
This isn't the exit.
It's the entrance!

16
00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:42.630
[laughing]

17
00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.160
[ALL yell]

18
00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:58.410
There it is!

19
00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:04.370
<b>God Land, Skypiea
White-White Sea</b>

20
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.920
Sky island!

21
00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.130
[USOPP, CHOPPER gasp]

22
00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:13.260
Wait. Didn't the sign
we passed say Skypiea?

23
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:14.260
[NAMI chuckles]

24
00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:16.020
[NAMI]
Yeah, that's the same name

25
00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:19.480
scribbled on the map
that Luffy found.

26
00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.730
So I guess that galleon
that fell on us

27
00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.610
actually did come here.

28
00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:26.900
The last time they set eyes
on this was 200 years ago.

29
00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.610
I'm amazed that this place has
stayed beautiful for so long.

30
00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:38.940
<b>Angel Beach</b>

31
00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:42.030
You can smell the adventure!
Potent as the seaweed in summer!

32
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.700
[USOPP]
Wait for me! I like adventure!

33
00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.250
It's kinda shallow.

34
00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.540
Usopp. Did you hurt yourself?

35
00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:50.750
Hello?

36
00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.550
Hey, is it just me, or is this
whole place made of clouds?

37
00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.340
Big fluffy ones!

38
00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:00.590
I love this stuff!

39
00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:02.890
[BOTH chuckle]

40
00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.310
C'mon! Let's go ashore!

41
00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:06.480
[USOPP]
Yeah!

42
00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:07.980
[BOTH laughing]

43
00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.520
Wait! I'm coming with you guys!

44
00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:12.750
Me, too. But I gotta
change first.

45
00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:15.670
Luffy, we may have a problem
with dropping anchor here.

46
00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.500
There's no seabed at the
bottom of this cloud.

47
00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.920
You know, I thought about
that, and who cares?!

48
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:20.710
[USOPP chuckles]

49
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:21.960
I love the way you think!

50
00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:22.760
Thanks!

51
00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.040
[BOTH laughing]

52
00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:27.210
[CHOPPER]
Me, too! I wanna play!

53
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.800
[LUFFY, USOPP, CHOPPER laughing]

54
00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.210
[LUFFY]
Hop on down and
check this out, you guys!

55
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.840
The beach is actually fluffy!

56
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.720
[USOPP]
It squishes between
my toes like marshmallow mud!

57
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.550
[LUFFY]
What the heck
is marshmallow mud?

58
00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:39.900
[USOPP]
I dunno,
but that's what it's like.

59
00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:42.900
Well, regardless of how
the ground may feel,

60
00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.110
it sure is an amazing sight.

61
00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:46.690
Better than I imagined it.

62
00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.140
It's a piece of work for sure,

63
00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:51.650
but I think our shipmates
are overreacting.

64
00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:52.860
[SANJI chuckles]

65
00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.490
[SANJI]
Bugs me when
they get all worked up.

66
00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.240
[laughing]

67
00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.160
[ZORO]
When they get all worked up?

68
00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:00.420
[thudding]

69
00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.530
[NAMI]
Ow! You're hurting
me! I said I was sorry!

70
00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.990
[NAMI]
Uh, we forgot
about the South Bird.

71
00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.120
We really should've let him go.

72
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.080
He'll never survive here.

73
00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.280
I wouldn't sweat it.

74
00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.560
Birds adapt very quickly
to their environments.

75
00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:19.310
And if not, there seem to be
some people living up here.

76
00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.690
Don't worry about
it. He'll be fine.

77
00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:23.270
Yeah. I guess.

78
00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:24.610
Hey, the anchor!

79
00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:26.170
All taken care of.

80
00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:27.710
It hit the heavier cloud below,

81
00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:29.800
which is what I think
this place floats on.

82
00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:31.750
[ROBIN]
That means that
sky island floats

83
00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.420
on a sea of cloudcover.

84
00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:35.410
I wonder if it's
stationary in the sky

85
00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:37.390
or if it moves with the tide.

86
00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.810
We could sit on deck all day
and pick this whole thing apart,

87
00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:43.410
but, come on, what
fun would that be?

88
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.460
Fact is, most of this
makes zero sense,

89
00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.460
so let's make the best of it.

90
00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:50.590
[laughs]

91
00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.090
[laughs]

92
00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:55.920
This is why we came
here, you guys!

93
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.470
Welcome to sky island!

94
00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:00.180
Come on! If I can do
this, so can you!

95
00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:03.640
Just jump!

96
00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.260
[ROBIN]
Swordsman, you joining us?

97
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.260
Yeah. I'll be along soon.

98
00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:13.310
The captain smells
adventure. Huh.

99
00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:17.650
I never stopped to think of
any of this as adventurous.

100
00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.570
Maybe I should
reconsider my stance.

101
00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.530
[splash]

102
00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:29.370
Something about the
squishiness of this island

103
00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.220
makes me wanna sleep all day.

104
00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:35.840
[LUFFY]
Ahoy! This is
your captain speaking!

105
00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.460
I must inform you that this
place is too much fun

106
00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:40.920
for me to make anymore
captain decisions.

107
00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:42.240
Aye, aye, Captain.

108
00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.830
What do you say we just
sit and do nothing? Eh?

109
00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:49.330
Nothing sounds like
a plan to me. Denied!

110
00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.170
[USOPP]
But "denied" means "no"!

111
00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:53.080
[SANJI]
Such mysterious-looking flowers.

112
00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.200
I've never seen
anything like them.

113
00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.040
[SANJI]
I wonder if they're balloon-ish

114
00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:01.460
like that sea monster,

115
00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:03.460
if they'll pop when
I pick one...

116
00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.170
This is great!

117
00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:11.260
Those Marine losers
can't follow us up here,

118
00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:13.640
so we can all cut loose.

119
00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.410
[NAMI]
Hey, Chopper. What do
you think that is over there?

120
00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:22.190
What?

121
00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.030
All right, Mr. Giant Fruit,
you're all mine...

122
00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:29.240
Ow, well, that hurt.

123
00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:31.400
Why's this thing so hard?

124
00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:32.570
Hey, Usopp!

125
00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:33.490
[USOPP]
Yeah?

126
00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:35.580
[LUFFY]
Got some fruit for ya!

127
00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:37.780
[USOPP]
You practically crammed
my brain into my chest!

128
00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:38.740
What's wrong with you?

129
00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:40.790
[LUFFY laughing]

130
00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:43.370
[SANJI]
Wait up, Robin, baby!

131
00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.670
I've got a present for you.
An island flower.

132
00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:50.050
And one, for, uh... Nami!

133
00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:51.300
Nami, baby!

134
00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:54.970
Where'd you run off to?!
I've got a gift for you!

135
00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:56.220
[NAMI]
Whoa.

136
00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:58.540
I can't believe I'm on
a chair made of cloud.

137
00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:01.500
I wonder is they have a factory
that makes all this stuff.

138
00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.800
This cloud is different from
the stuff on the beach.

139
00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:05.210
It's a lot spongier.

140
00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:06.460
[SANJI]
Nami!

141
00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:07.960
[ZORO]
I didn't figure on gettin' wet

142
00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:09.630
walkin' through clouds.

143
00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:11.760
Huh? What's that?

144
00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:15.510
[SUU]
Suu! Suu!

145
00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:17.450
[ZORO]
Hm?

146
00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:18.560
[ZORO yelps]

147
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:20.680
[ZORO]
Sorry.

148
00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:24.550
Huh. And what do we have
here? Some kinda fox?

149
00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:27.670
[LUFFY]
Huh?

150
00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:29.760
Is that music?

151
00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.680
Look. There's
someone over there.

152
00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:34.890
On that cloud!

153
00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:37.390
[growls]
Oh, great.
It's another monster.

154
00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:38.940
[CHOPPER]
Where's the whistle?
Where's the whistle?

155
00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:41.350
We can ask the Sky
Knight to save us!

156
00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:42.690
No, we can't.

157
00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:45.110
That's not who you think
it is. That right there...

158
00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:46.630
...is an angel.

159
00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:49.300
An angel?

160
00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:56.050
Heso.

161
00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:58.200
[BOTH]
– Heso?
– Heso?

162
00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.010
Suu. Come here.

163
00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:00.890
[SUU]
Suu!

164
00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:03.180
[CONIS]
Did you come
from the Blue Sea World?

165
00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:04.390
[LUFFY]
If that's what you call it.

166
00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:06.730
We sailed up a river
of clouds to get here.

167
00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:09.100
Speaking of "here,"
is this is your home?

168
00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:11.230
[CONIS]
Yes, it sure is.

169
00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:15.350
And I'd like to welcome you
to Skypiea's Angel Beach.

170
00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:18.070
I bet you'd like to drink that.

171
00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:21.490
[LUFFY]
Mh-hm.

172
00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:24.120
The top of a conush
is as hard as steel.

173
00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:25.540
It's impossible to crack,

174
00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:28.920
so you have to cut through
the underside, like this.

175
00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:32.350
[CONIS]
Here ya go.

176
00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:34.150
[SUU]
Suu.

177
00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:40.690
If it's that good,
I gotta try some.

178
00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:49.810
<b>Conis & Cloud Fox Suu
Angel Beach, Skypiea Residents</b>

179
00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:41.680
C'mon, give it to me!

180
00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:43.770
[USOPP slurping]

181
00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:48.300
My name is Conis, and
this little one is Suu.

182
00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:49.840
She's a cloud fox.

183
00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:52.940
If you need anything at all,
please let me be of service.

184
00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.590
Yeah, about that.

185
00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:57.950
Your eyes are setting
my heart on fi...!

186
00:09:58.010 --> 00:09:59.510
Lay off.

187
00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:02.010
Actually, we've got
tons of questions.

188
00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:03.290
I hate to bother you,

189
00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:05.960
but we've never been any
place like this before.

190
00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:08.220
Sure, ask me anything
you wanna know.

191
00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:10.920
Guys, look. Something's coming.

192
00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:12.500
Oh, that's my father.

193
00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:14.720
[PAGAYA]
Heso, Conis!

194
00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.080
[CONIS]
Heso, Father!

195
00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:19.720
Can't you say hello
like normal people?

196
00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:20.880
[NAMI gasps]

197
00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:22.800
Conis, what's that
thing he's riding on?

198
00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:25.430
Ah, that would be
my father's waver.

199
00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.020
[LUFFY]
Whoa! That's awesome!

200
00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:28.610
A sky waver.

201
00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.610
[PAGAYA]
Gang way! Stopping
this thing is a bit of a trick!

202
00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:36.910
[PAGAYA yelping]

203
00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:41.500
[groans]

204
00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:44.050
[PAGAYA]
My goodness.
I-Is everyone all right?

205
00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:46.220
You're the one who crashed!

206
00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:49.090
Hey, Luffy, didn't you bring
back something like that waver

207
00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:50.100
from the seabed?

208
00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:51.910
Uh, yeah. I did, didn't I?

209
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:53.250
Huh.

210
00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:55.000
Then that must be the
boat we read about

211
00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:56.670
in that "Noland, the Liar" book.

212
00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:59.000
A boat that can
sail without wind.

213
00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:01.510
Did you make some new
friends, Conis, my dear?

214
00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:03.050
[CONIS]
Yes.

215
00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:05.380
They arrived only a few minutes
ago from the Blue Sea World.

216
00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:07.120
[PAGAYA]
Oh, still adjusting.

217
00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:08.950
There must be a great many
things that puzzle you

218
00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:10.510
about the White White Sea.

219
00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:11.570
I'm sorry.

220
00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:13.030
[USOPP]
There's no need to apologize.

221
00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:15.600
[PAGAYA]
Forgive me for not
introducing myself sooner.

222
00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:16.850
My name is Pagaya.

223
00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:18.930
[USOPP]
Oh, it's, uh,
very nice to meet you.

224
00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.270
So, Father, how was
the fishing today?

225
00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.860
[PAGAYA]
Quite a successful venture.

226
00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.750
I have an idea. You all
should come over for dinner.

227
00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:29.300
We'll fill your bellies with
the taste of the island.

228
00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:31.680
I'm all for that! I'm starving!

229
00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:33.720
Sky island cuisine?

230
00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:35.240
Do you mind if I help
you prepare it?

231
00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:36.610
[PAGAYA]
No!

232
00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:38.660
Before we go, I have a question.

233
00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:41.620
This boat, it doesn't have
any sails to catch the wind

234
00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.410
and you weren't
rowing it either,

235
00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:45.850
so how is it propelled
through the water?

236
00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:47.540
Oh, of course.

237
00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:50.170
Your people aren't familiar
with dials are they?

238
00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:51.920
What're dials?

239
00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:56.010
[screams]

240
00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:59.480
[screaming]

241
00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:01.110
[LUFFY]
Why won't it stop?!

242
00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:04.060
[LUFFY screams]

243
00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:06.490
[CONIS]
Oh, no! I hope he's all right!

244
00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:07.740
It's all my fault!

245
00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:10.660
I shouldn't have let a
rookie use the waver.

246
00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:11.910
Since Luffy can't swim,

247
00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:14.080
I hope this sea is more
buoyant than ours.

248
00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:15.660
Maybe.

249
00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:18.540
If it can stay floating in the
air, then you're probably right.

250
00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:19.370
He can handle it.

251
00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:21.450
[LUFFY gargling]

252
00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:22.880
[SANJI]
Huh? He sank.

253
00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:24.520
[ZORO]
Eh, it's not the first
time I've been wrong.

254
00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:27.530
[CHOPPER]
Ah! I'll save you!

255
00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:28.730
[SANJI]
That was close.

256
00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:30.610
[ZORO]
Close? You were
standing on the beach!

257
00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:32.400
What do you know about "close"?

258
00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:35.150
Oh, yeah, and thanks for
all the help, by the way!

259
00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:37.570
[USOPP]
Was it really necessary
for you to jump in too?

260
00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:39.940
Sky island scary.
Sky island scary.

261
00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.410
I apologize for letting
him take the waver out.

262
00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:45.250
With its light body,
even a small wave

263
00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:46.830
can shake the rudder.

264
00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:49.410
Unless you know the White White
Sea like the back of your hand,

265
00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.920
the waver is almost
impossible to ride.

266
00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.750
Again, I am sorry.

267
00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:56.550
It's only recently that I
became good enough to ride it.

268
00:12:56.630 --> 00:12:58.760
Whoa! Is it really that hard?

269
00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.640
It usually takes ten years
of training to drive it.

270
00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:03.160
You gotta be joking!

271
00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:05.450
Wow! Look at her go!

272
00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:06.670
[NAMI]
This is wonderful!

273
00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.300
Oh, Nami! You're a vision
riding that waver!

274
00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:10.510
That's incredible!

275
00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:12.800
I can't believe it!
This is her first time?

276
00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:15.460
Yeah! How come she can
do it and I can't?!

277
00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:18.320
[NAMI]
Wow! This really requires skill!

278
00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:20.840
No wonder you had
a hard time Luffy!

279
00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:22.970
[NAMI laughing]

280
00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:24.680
[LUFFY]
Nami, we're invited to dinner,

281
00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:28.260
so get off that thing
and stop being so rude!

282
00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:29.370
[SANJI]
Talk about rude.

283
00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:30.850
[LUFFY]
Stupid waver hog!

284
00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:34.970
If it's okay with Pagaya, I'd
like to stay out here and ride.

285
00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:37.520
[PAGAYA]
That's fine. Just be careful.

286
00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:39.650
[NAMI]
Riding this freely
on the open ocean

287
00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:42.610
without worrying about
the wind is like a dream!

288
00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:45.700
I wonder if we can use these
on our ocean back home.

289
00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.290
Whoa. Is that some
kinda factory?

290
00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:51.460
[CHOPPER]
Hm?

291
00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:52.880
[PAGAYA]
Indeed it is.

292
00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:54.920
That is our cloud quarry site.

293
00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:56.920
It's where our clouds
are manufactured

294
00:13:57.010 --> 00:13:59.300
and cut to meet all
types of needs.

295
00:13:59.390 --> 00:14:00.850
[ZORO]
I didn't know
you could cut a cloud.

296
00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:02.340
[PAGAYA]
You did take the Milky Road

297
00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:04.600
to get to the White
White Sea, correct?

298
00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:06.140
[ZORO]
Milky Road?

299
00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:07.580
Is that the name of that messed
up current we took up here?

300
00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:08.950
[PAGAYA]
Yes.

301
00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:10.710
It's a canal constructed
by our engineers

302
00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:13.960
and made using the natural
clouds we quarry.

303
00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:16.820
There are actually two types of
natural clouds on our island.

304
00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:19.950
Your ship traveled atop
what we call sea clouds.

305
00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:21.500
And the ones that
float on their own,

306
00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:25.880
that we can stand on and build
on, are called island clouds.

307
00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:28.960
They're not the normal clouds
that we're used to, are they?

308
00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:30.090
[PAGAYA]
No.

309
00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:31.670
Their condensation
nucleus is different

310
00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:33.880
from what you usually see.

311
00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:36.380
It has to do with a mineral
found in the Blue Sea World

312
00:14:36.470 --> 00:14:38.760
called Sea Prism Stone.

313
00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:39.970
Sea Prism Stone?

314
00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:41.510
What affect does that
have on the clouds?

315
00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:42.720
[PAGAYA]
It's like this.

316
00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:45.160
Inside the Sea Prism
are carnelian particles

317
00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:46.660
called pyrobrine.

318
00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:48.330
During volcanic eruptions,

319
00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:51.910
these particles gain moisture
and ascend into the sky.

320
00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:55.720
Our clouds are formed based on
the density of the moisture.

321
00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:57.800
Oh, yeah. I heard
about those things.

322
00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:01.170
Sure. I remember that
from when I was a kid.

323
00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:04.140
We used to always play with
carnelian particles in the yard.

324
00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:06.430
Enough sarcasm!

325
00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:07.940
In all actuality,

326
00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:10.770
there are three types of clouds
that we use in our day to day.

327
00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:12.940
The sea and island
types are natural.

328
00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:15.530
The man-made ones come
from island clouds.

329
00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:18.010
Now if you'll follow me, please.

330
00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:20.140
[LUFFY]
Check out the
view from up here!

331
00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:21.470
[CHOPPER]
Look!

332
00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:23.150
If you squint, you can see
Nami riding the waver!

333
00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:25.820
[SANJI]
Luffy, what are you waiting on?

334
00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.620
How come she's so good
at that stupid thing?

335
00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:30.870
Fall off!

336
00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:32.300
[SANJI]
Cut it out!

337
00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.180
[LUFFY]
Thanks, Sanji!
You just made me bite my tongue!

338
00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:35.150
I'm bleeding!

339
00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:36.270
[SANJI]
Poor baby.

340
00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:37.870
Hey, Pagaya, which
way to the kitchen?

341
00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:39.960
[PAGAYA]
It's right up here.

342
00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:44.670
[coughing]

343
00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:47.330
What happened to you?

344
00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:51.140
Well, I saw this stuff sitting
in your fridge, and I, uh...

345
00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:55.360
I'd never seen anything like it,
so I tried it, and... and...

346
00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:56.660
It tastes like death.

347
00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:59.250
Now I'm all dizzy and
my tongue feels swollen.

348
00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:00.420
How's it look?

349
00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:02.170
Like you drank some
spoiled sauce.

350
00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:04.260
[SANJI]
Then why did you leave
this stuff sitting around?

351
00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:05.540
If something's old,
you throw it...

352
00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:07.630
It sounds like they're
havin' fun in the kitchen.

353
00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:10.210
I bet Sanji's just excited
about sky island food

354
00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:12.700
being such a new thing.

355
00:16:12.790 --> 00:16:15.210
So, uh, I was wondering.

356
00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:16.580
Could you explain
some more to us

357
00:16:16.660 --> 00:16:18.620
about these dials you
mentioned earlier?

358
00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:20.080
[CONIS]
Sure.

359
00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:23.750
But it's complex. I don't
exactly know where to start.

360
00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:29.220
Ah. I've never been
this relaxed.

361
00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:30.630
[LUFFY]
Hm.

362
00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:33.140
This doesn't look like a dial.
It's just a seashell.

363
00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:34.850
Yes, at first glance,

364
00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:36.520
but why don't you put it
to your mouth and talk?

365
00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:38.450
Usopp stinks.

366
00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:40.280
Why do you always pick on me?

367
00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:41.620
[CONIS giggles]

368
00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:45.190
[CONIS]
Now just press
down the apex of the shell.

369
00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:47.250
Wanna tell me what an apex is?

370
00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:49.340
It means vertex
or highest point.

371
00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:51.110
[LUFFY]
Usopp stinks!

372
00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:53.690
Ah! The seashell's makin'
fun of Usopp, too!

373
00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:55.660
What'd I ever do
to you, seashell?!

374
00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:57.070
[CONIS]
...press down
the apex of the shell.

375
00:16:57.070 --> 00:16:59.120
Hey, that's a neat trick.

376
00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:01.160
So it can store any
sound and play it back?

377
00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:03.040
Then that shell is
one of the dials.

378
00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:05.790
Yes. We call this
one a tone dial.

379
00:17:05.870 --> 00:17:07.540
It is a shell from
the White White Sea

380
00:17:07.620 --> 00:17:10.170
that records and replays
whatever it hears.

381
00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:11.710
[LUFFY]
Ah.

382
00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:14.510
Hey, hold on. I didn't think
there was a bottom to your sea.

383
00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:16.920
Where do these
things live anyway?

384
00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:18.930
They live in the shallows
in cloud reefs.

385
00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:21.150
I still don't see
how that little thing

386
00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:23.160
could move your
father's sky waver.

387
00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:26.420
Well, that's because this is
the kind that powers the waver.

388
00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:29.450
It's smaller, but it does
demonstrate the same process.

389
00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:31.420
Here. Let me show you.

390
00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:35.380
[LUFFY, USOPP]
– Ohh.
– Ohh.

391
00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:37.420
[USOPP]
So this one blows
wind out of its backside.

392
00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:38.680
Lemme see.

393
00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:41.850
This is called a breath dial.

394
00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:43.810
It allows you the ability
to store the wind

395
00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:45.710
and expel it at your desire.

396
00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:48.380
[yells]

397
00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:50.260
[CONIS]
The wind capacity
varies depending on

398
00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:51.670
the size of the shell.

399
00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.440
If you attach the right size
to the stern of a ship,

400
00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:56.220
you can move along swiftly.

401
00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:59.310
I've never heard of
anything like that.

402
00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:00.600
I get it now.

403
00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:01.850
The waver moved by blowing

404
00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:03.520
a stream of wind
through the water.

405
00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:06.810
That waver is fun.

406
00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:08.980
Dials don't just power wavers.

407
00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:11.770
There's also the skating kind,
the board kind.

408
00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:13.110
But I don't go for those.

409
00:18:13.190 --> 00:18:14.940
The waver is about
as crazy as I get.

410
00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.850
[LUFFY]
I wish I was out there
on that thing instead of Nami.

411
00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:19.350
And I could be if I
could just figure out

412
00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:20.730
how to fix the one we have.

413
00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:23.070
How did you ever find
a waver in your world?

414
00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:25.070
Well, it's kinda
hard to even tell

415
00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:27.050
if that's what it is, you know.

416
00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:29.550
That hunk of junk is 200
years old and a total wreck.

417
00:18:29.630 --> 00:18:31.050
There's no way it works.

418
00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:33.800
[CONIS]
That might not be the case.

419
00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:36.510
Dials are made from the
remains of dead shellfish.

420
00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:37.720
They can last indefinitely

421
00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:39.770
so long as the shell
itself isn't broken.

422
00:18:39.850 --> 00:18:40.890
You might get lucky.

423
00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:42.100
I told ya so.

424
00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:43.370
[USOPP]
You never said
anything like that.

425
00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:44.920
[LUFFY]
I know.

426
00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.750
[ROBIN]
I'd venture a guess
there are many types of dials.

427
00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:48.790
Is this one here?

428
00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:51.170
Yes. That's a lamp dial.

429
00:18:52.250 --> 00:18:54.550
Neat! It lit up from inside!

430
00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:57.720
[CONIS]
Right. There are flame
dials that store heat and fire.

431
00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:00.510
Flavor dials that store
various tastes and aromas.

432
00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:03.160
And wision dials that store
everything the user sees,

433
00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.240
which you can watch
again and again.

434
00:19:05.390 --> 00:19:09.140
I wanna live in the clouds
for the rest of my life.

435
00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:11.920
To be honest, we couldn't really
sustain life here in the clouds

436
00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:13.010
without these dials.

437
00:19:13.090 --> 00:19:14.090
Huh.

438
00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:17.510
Our culture has evolved
with the dials.

439
00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:19.300
I suppose in the same
way they evolved

440
00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:20.970
with the White White Sea.

441
00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:24.060
[snoring]

442
00:19:30.530 --> 00:19:32.240
[SANJI]
Ladies and gentlemen,
dinner is served.

443
00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:34.610
We have for you sky lobster
with special sauce

444
00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:36.780
and a side of island fruits
that I can't pronounce.

445
00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:38.030
[LUFFY]
That looks so good!

446
00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:39.820
Let's dig in!

447
00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:44.780
[LUFFY]
Oh, wow! It tastes sweet!

448
00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:47.410
[USOPP]
I wouldn't have
expected it from lobster.

449
00:19:47.450 --> 00:19:48.870
[LUFFY]
Yum!

450
00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:55.800
Hey, guys. Any of you
know where Nami is?

451
00:19:55.920 --> 00:19:58.260
I think she's riding the waver.

452
00:19:58.340 --> 00:19:59.600
I don't see her.

453
00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:01.550
She probably went out
too far. She's like that.

454
00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:04.800
Who cares? You should
have some of this!

455
00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:07.090
I'm worried. Do you
think she's okay?

456
00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:10.980
I have my concerns, but I can't
say one way or the other.

457
00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:13.400
Uh, what're you talkin' about?

458
00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:16.030
In the clouds, there's a
place called the Upper Yard

459
00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:18.370
that no one is ever allowed
to set step foot on.

460
00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:20.410
It's not too far away.

461
00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:23.490
It would only be a short
trip by waver from here.

462
00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:24.740
I'm afraid your friend Nami

463
00:20:24.830 --> 00:20:27.290
might've set ashore there
out of curiousity.

464
00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:33.960
They're huge. What is this?

465
00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:35.050
[USOPP]
What's so bad that no one

466
00:20:35.130 --> 00:20:37.180
can ever go over to this island?

467
00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:39.350
Is it full of monsters?

468
00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:41.050
It's sacred ground.

469
00:20:41.050 --> 00:20:42.270
Sacred ground?

470
00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:45.730
But it's so much more
than just that.

471
00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:50.270
It's the island...
where God lives.
